WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.348
I was spying on the pirates' meeting through a musket hole.

2
00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:19.041
"They have a knife and a book," I said.

3
00:00:19.042 --> 00:00:23.407
The group started walking back to the house. "Here they come."

4
00:00:23.408 --> 00:00:28.398
"Let them come, lad," Long John Silver said cheerily.

5
00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:32.634
The five men came in and pushed one man forward.

6
00:00:32.635 --> 00:00:38.934
"Hand it over, lad," Long John cried. "I know the rules."

7
00:00:38.935 --> 00:00:42.498
The pirate passed something into Long John's hand.

8
00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:48.443
"The black spot! I thought so," he said, looking down.

9
00:00:48.444 --> 00:00:58.284
"Where did you get the paper? Don't tell me you've cut this piece out of a Bible. What fool would cut up a Bible?"

10
00:00:58.285 --> 00:01:06.785
"I told you swabs," yelled Tom Morgan, "we shouldn't use a Bible! Nothing good will come of doing that!"

11
00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:15.682
"You'll all hang now, I reckon," remarked Long John. "What stupid swab had a Bible?"

12
00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:18.711
"It was Dick," someone mumbled.

13
00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:25.165
"Well, Dick's luck is cursed now," said Long John.

14
00:01:25.166 --> 00:01:34.790
The tall pirate stepped forward. "This crew has given you the black spot," he said. "Read it. Then you can talk."

15
00:01:34.791 --> 00:01:41.324
"Ah, George Merry," Long John replied. "Let me see.

16
00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:48.460
It says 'Deposed'—very well written. Is it your handwriting, George?

17
00:01:48.461 --> 00:01:53.450
Why, you'll be captain next, I guess."

18
00:01:53.451 --> 00:01:56.905
"Don't try to be funny," George growled.

19
00:01:56.906 --> 00:02:01.664
"You're done being captain. Stand up and vote."

20
00:02:01.665 --> 00:02:08.804
"I thought you knew the rules!" shouted Long John. "But if you don't, I do!

21
00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:19.259
Your black spot isn't worth anything unless you state your complaints and I reply. After that, we'll see."

22
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.217
"First, you've ruined this voyage," said George.

23
00:02:24.218 --> 00:02:34.199
"Second, you let the enemy escape. Third, you wouldn't let us chase them. And then, fourth, there's this boy."

24
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.946
"Is that all?" Long John asked quietly.

25
00:02:37.947 --> 00:02:46.802
"That's enough," George retorted. "We'll all swing and dry in the sun because of your blunders."

26
00:02:46.803 --> 00:02:51.964
"I ruined this voyage, did I?" bellowed Long John.

27
00:02:51.965 --> 00:03:03.957
"Well, if things had been done my way, we'd be aboard the Hispaniola tonight, every man alive and well, with the treasure in the hold, by thunder!

28
00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:13.077
But who crossed me? It was Job Anderson, Israel Hands, and now you, George Merry!

29
00:03:13.078 --> 00:03:19.537
And you have the nerve to complain? You sank all of us!"

30
00:03:19.538 --> 00:03:25.541
Long John Silver paused, and I could see by the men's faces that they were listening intently.

31
00:03:25.542 --> 00:03:31.021
"That's number one," Long John yelled, wiping the sweat from his brow.

32
00:03:31.022 --> 00:03:36.563
"I'm sick of you. You've neither sense nor memory.

33
00:03:36.564 --> 00:03:42.364
You aren't pirates—you're a bunch of ladies at a dance!"

34
00:03:42.365 --> 00:03:46.639
"Watch your tongue, Long John," said Tom Morgan.

35
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.258
"You say this voyage is ruined," Long John continued.

36
00:03:51.259 --> 00:03:58.728
"It is! And we're so near the gallows, my neck is stiff just thinking about it.

37
00:03:58.729 --> 00:04:05.278
And it's thanks to Merry, Hands, Anderson, and the other fools."

38
00:04:05.279 --> 00:04:11.072
"Go on," said Tom Morgan. "What about the other points?"

39
00:04:11.073 --> 00:04:21.192
"Number two? You all came crawling to me to make peace with the others—and you'd have starved too if I hadn't.

40
00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:27.283
Number three? There's a great deal to say about number three.

41
00:04:27.284 --> 00:04:32.570
Maybe you don't want a real doctor treating you every day—

42
00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:43.114
you, with your head broke—or you, George Merry, who was ill just six hours ago, your eyes the color of a lemon.

43
00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:51.579
And number four! That boy! Shiver my timbers, he's a hostage!

44
00:04:51.580 --> 00:05:01.155
Do you want to waste a hostage who might be our last chance? Kill that boy? Not me, mates!

45
00:05:01.156 --> 00:05:06.401
And perhaps you don't know, but there's another ship on the way!

46
00:05:06.402 --> 00:05:11.018
Won't we be glad to have a hostage when it comes?

47
00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:17.522
But that's a minor reason. Look there—that's why!"

48
00:05:17.523 --> 00:05:23.767
And he threw down a paper I instantly recognized—the map I'd found in the captain's chest.

49
00:05:23.768 --> 00:05:28.407
"Why had the doctor given it to Long John Silver?" I wondered.

50
00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:31.395
"That's the map!" someone yelled.

51
00:05:31.396 --> 00:05:34.493
"Let me see it!" another shouted.

52
00:05:34.494 --> 00:05:39.580
They jumped on it like cats on a mouse, passing it back and forth.

53
00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:42.657
"It's beautiful," Dick cried.

54
00:05:42.658 --> 00:05:46.578
They acted as if they were counting the gold coins already.

55
00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:50.666
"That's Flint's writing," observed Tom Morgan.

56
00:05:50.667 --> 00:05:58.415
"Mighty pretty," said George. "But how do we get away with the treasure if we have no ship?"

57
00:05:58.416 --> 00:06:04.366
Long John Silver sprang up. "Oh, so now, you want my opinion?

58
00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:08.526
Well, I don't want to hear another word from any of you!

59
00:06:08.527 --> 00:06:15.667
You, George Merry, and the rest who stupidly lost the ship, can go hang!

60
00:06:15.668 --> 00:06:22.451
None of you has the brain of a cockroach, so you can just keep your mouths shut."

61
00:06:22.452 --> 00:06:26.995
"Now, Long John—," said Tom Morgan.

62
00:06:26.996 --> 00:06:33.831
"You lost the ship, I found the treasure," Long John roared.

63
00:06:33.832 --> 00:06:43.966
"Who's the better man? And now, I resign, by thunder! Elect whom you please. I'm done."

64
00:06:43.967 --> 00:06:49.993
"Long John Silver!" they cried. "Silver for captain!"

65
00:06:49.994 --> 00:06:54.479
"So now you want me?" Long John bellowed.

66
00:06:54.480 --> 00:07:05.278
"What about this black spot? It's not much good now. Dick spoiled his luck for absolutely nothing."

67
00:07:05.279 --> 00:07:10.555
"Can I kiss the Bible to take away the curse?" asked Dick hopefully.

68
00:07:10.556 --> 00:07:19.949
"A Bible with a page cut out," Silver said scornfully, "is no better than a songbook, lad."

69
00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:27.985
Long John turned and handed me the piece of paper. "Here, Jim—keep this as a souvenir."

70
00:07:27.986 --> 00:07:35.355
The paper was round. One side was blackened with wood ash and had a Bible verse that contained these words:

71
00:07:35.356 --> 00:07:43.534
"Outside are dogs and murderers." The other side was blank, except for the word "Deposed," written on it.

72
00:07:43.535 --> 00:07:47.220
I still have that souvenir as I write this tale.

73
00:07:47.221 --> 00:07:55.512
"George," Long John said, "guard the house—and do a good job or we'll kill you."

74
00:07:55.513 --> 00:07:59.759
Soon after, we lay down, but I couldn't sleep.

75
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.868
Tossing and turning, I could see the remarkable game Long John was playing—

76
00:08:04.869 --> 00:08:10.747
keeping the pirates together with one hand, and trying to save his miserable life with the other.

77
00:08:10.748 --> 00:08:20.993
But Long John peacefully snored, and as danger loomed all around us, all I could see was the gallows awaiting him.
